OVERVIEW. The page starts a table of Briggsian (base-10) logarithms of Gauss's factorial function Π(z) = Γ(z+1), to about 20-25 decimals, for z ≡ ±1/4 (mod 1). The starting value comes from the lemniscatic constant. Here 'ϖ' means the quarter-period ϖ/2 = ∫_0^1 dx/√(1−x^4) = 1.3110287771460599…, not the full 2.622… of the Disquisitiones. This is clear because log ϖ = 0.11761… = log10(1.31103).

(A) Computing log(ϖ/2) to about 25 places (upper half of the page, heading 'Berechnung von log ϖ').
1. Start with ϖ/2 = 1.311028777146059 87.
2. Multiply by 0.76275 = 339·225/10^5, done as 0.75 × 1.017:
   • ×3 gives 3.93308633143817961;
   • /4 gives 0.98327158285954490;
   • ×0.016 gives 0.0157323453257527 18;
   • ×0.001 gives 0.00098327158285954 5;
   • the sum is 0.99998719976815716 5.
3. Multiply by 1.000013, adding the ×10^-5 and ×3·10^-6 parts. This gives the residual factor 1+x = 1.00000019960175415 2, with x ≈ 1.99601754·10^-7.
4. Hence log(ϖ/2) = 5 − log 76275 − log 1.000013 + log(1+x). The pieces:
   • log 339 = 2.53019 96982 03082 16009;
   • log 225 = 2.35218 25181 11362 48416;
   • their sum is log 76275 = 4.88238 22163 14444 64425;
   • log 1.000013 = 0.0000056457 91567 17669;
   • these combine to log(76275·1.000013) = 4.88238 78621 06…, which is subtracted from 5.
   A first attempt with the wrong sign (0.11762 34294…) is struck out.
5. log(1+x) ≈ M(x − x²/2), with M = log e:
   • Mx is formed from 2M = 0.868588963806503 65 times 0.99800877076, built as 1 − 0.002 + 0.00000875 + 0.00000002 + …; this gives Mx = 8.66859404064 23·10^-8;
   • M x²/2 ≈ 8.65133·10^-15 is subtracted, giving log(1+x) = 8.66859317 5509·10^-8.
6. Result: log ϖ = 0.11761 22245 79919 93424. Gauss marks 'wirklicher Rechnungsfehler' (real computing error) and corrects the tail to 94762, then to 94586. Final value: log ϖ = 0.11761 22245 79919 94586 18851 58564.

(B) Starting value log Π(1/4).
1. ϖ/2 = Γ(1/4)²/(4√(2π)) and Γ(5/4) = Γ(1/4)/4 together give Π(1/4) = π^{1/4} (ϖ/2)^{1/2} / 8^{1/4} (my reconstruction).
2. On the page:
   • log(π^{1/4}ϖ^{1/2}) = ½·log√π + ½·log ϖ = 0.18309 35804 63493 43651 87610, using log√π = 0.24857 49363 47066 92717 56368;
   • log ∜8 = ¾·log 2 = 0.22577 24967 47985 89641 03042;
   • difference: log Π(0.25) = 9.95732 10837 15507 54010 84568 (−10).

(C) log Π(−1/4) = log Γ(3/4).
1. Reflection: Π(z)Π(−z) = πz/sin πz. At z = 1/4 this gives Π(1/4)Π(−1/4) = π√2/4.
2. The auxiliary line 0.13096 27117… equals log(π√2/4) − 2·log Π(1/4) = log(Π(−¼)/Π(¼)).
3. Adding log Π(1/4) gives Π(−0.25) = 0.08828 37954 82654 52142 22085.

(D) Recurrence Π(z+1) = (z+1)·Π(z) in logarithms.
• Left column: add log 0.75, 1.75, 2.75, … successively to get log Π(0.75), Π(1.75), …, Π(4.75). Log 5.75 is written ready for the next page.
• Right column: add log 1.25, 2.25, …, 12.25 to get log Π(1.25) through log Π(12.25) = 8.95567 13910 13801 50806.
• The next page continues with the 0.75-values and the half-integers. It then assembles a difference table of log Π from 8 to 13 in steps of 0.25, presumably for interpolation or checking against Stirling's series. This belongs with the tables of log Π in 'Disquisitiones generales circa seriem infinitam' (1812).
